\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Measurement error.}
Classical measurement error theory shows that correction via estimated error models can amplify MSE when the error structure is misspecified~\cite{fuller1987measurement,carroll2006measurement}.
Three distinctions separate our contribution: (i)~we study effect modifier misclassification---the mismeasured variable defines the CATE function itself---rather than regressor~\cite{frazis2003estimating} or confounder~\cite{ogburn2012nondifferential} error; (ii)~Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} characterizes correction-induced bias (bias created by the debiasing procedure), not attenuation from misclassification; (iii)~the $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau$ product structure arises only under heterogeneous error rates, revealing sign reversal invisible to any global-scalar analysis~\cite{battistin2014treatment,fong2021machine}.
\citet{frazis2003estimating} derive scalar attenuation $\lambda = (1-2\pi)$ for homogeneous errors; Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} generalizes this to subgroup-heterogeneous settings where correction can reverse bias sign across subgroups (Appendix~\ref{app:fl_comparison}).

\paragraph{HTE estimation and LLM annotation.}
Causal forests~\cite{athey2016recursive,wager2018causal}, debiased ML~\cite{chernozhukov2018dml}, and meta-learners~\cite{kunzel2019metalearners} assume covariates defining heterogeneity are observed without error.
LLMs now match crowd-worker accuracy on many classification tasks~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt,tornberg2024chatgpt}, but error rates vary by up to $5\times$ across subgroups~\cite{ashwin2025bias}; this distorts the contrasts that define the research question.
For causal inference with text, see \citet{feder2022causal,keith2020text,veitch2020adapting,wood2018challenges}; \citet{wang2020methods} study inference correction when outcomes are ML-predicted.

\paragraph{Prediction-powered and design-based correction.}
PPI~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi,zrnic2024cross} and DSL~\cite{egami2023dsl} correct population-level aggregates assuming homogeneous prediction error; pooled DSL is equivalent to our Global baseline (Appendix~\ref{app:ppi_comparison}).
\citet{mozer2026ppi} extend PPI to arm-specific ATE; PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci} corrects outcome-side error; ForestIV~\cite{bu2023forestiv} targets the outcome model.
None addresses bias when the mismeasured variable is the effect modifier indexing the CATE function.

\paragraph{Noisy label learning.}
The $M^{-1}$ correction structure originates in the label noise literature~\cite{natarajan2013learning,patrini2017making}; \citet{cheng2020learning} extend to instance-dependent noise.
Our subgroup-dependent error rates lie between class-conditional noise~\citep{natarajan2013learning} and fully instance-dependent noise~\citep{xia2020part}; unlike the latter, per-subgroup estimation is tractable with limited expert labels.
These methods correct \emph{training losses}; we correct \emph{causal estimands}, and the heterogeneity-driven bias identity has no analogue in that literature.

